BEGIN;

-- non-controlling-interest-control-attribution-and-ownership-changes
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Consolidation adds 100 percent of a controlled subsidiary's assets, liabilities, income and expenses, even when the parent owns less than all of it. The non-controlling interest (NCI) is the outside owners' share of that subsidiary's equity. It is equity of the group, presented separately from the parent's owners' equity, and it absorbs its share of profit, other comprehensive income (OCI) and losses, even into a deficit.</p></li><li><p>At acquisition the acquirer chooses, deal by deal, to measure NCI at fair value or at its proportionate share of the identifiable net assets (PSAK 103). At the acquisition date the choice changes goodwill and NCI by the same amount and nothing else.</p></li><li><p>While control lasts, buying from or selling to the NCI is a transaction between owners. No gain or loss is recognised, no new goodwill arises, and the difference goes to the parent's equity. Only gaining or losing control is remeasured through profit or loss.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report non-controlling interest of 674,235 at 31 December 2024 against 644,429 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 4.5 percent of total equity (4.5 percent a year earlier) and 2.2 percent of total assets (2.1 percent). Of the year's profit of 3,121,035, 119,560 is attributed to NCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15).</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to measure NCI at acquisition, roll it forward through profit, OCI and dividends, record a change in ownership with and without loss of control, compute NCI in a chain of holdings, and read a group's NCI disclosures.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What a non-controlling interest is</h2><p>NCI exists because consolidation is all-or-nothing on the asset side and proportionate on the ownership side. PSAK 110 <em>Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian</em> (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) defines it as equity in a subsidiary not attributable, directly or indirectly, to a parent.</p><p>The lecture notes (AKL Chapter 3) explain why the group is reported as one entity. Separate legal companies under one management are one economic unit, so their statements are combined line by line and the parent's investment account is replaced by the subsidiary's assets and liabilities. At 90 percent ownership all of the subsidiary's cash is still consolidated, and the outsiders' 10 percent is shown as a claim inside equity.</p><p><strong>Control decides who consolidates.</strong> The notes date from the old PSAK 4 era and say control is usually obtained through "hak mayoritas (lebih dari 50%)", with exceptions for indirect holdings. PSAK 110 replaced that with three elements, all of which must be present: power over the investee's relevant activities, exposure or rights to variable returns, and the ability to use that power to affect those returns. PHE's policy repeats them (Note 3c, Daftar 5/33, PDF p. 50) and adds that, with less than a majority of votes, it considers contractual arrangements, other contractual rights and potential voting rights (Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51). Majority ownership is the usual route to control, but the test is the three elements. A 45 percent holder whose remaining shares are spread among thousands of small holders may control; a 60 percent holder whose shareholder agreement gives the 40 percent holder a veto over the budget may not.</p><p>The notes' PSAK 4 exclusions, temporary control and restrictions on transferring funds, no longer exist; a subsidiary held for sale is consolidated under PSAK 105 (IFRS 5).</p><p><strong>NCI is equity.</strong> The notes first place NCI "pada bagian sesi liabilities ... dibawah heading non current liabilities" and then cite GAAP: NCI "harus ditampilkan dalam balancesheet sebagai bagian yang terpisah dari komponen equity", which can be misread as outside equity. The current rule puts it within equity, separately from the parent's owners' equity (PSAK 110 para. 22). The outside shareholders own residual interests that nobody owes them, so they are not a liability. The same logic places NCI's share of profit below profit for the year as an attribution, not as an expense.</p><blockquote><p>Profit for the year = profit attributable to owners of the parent + profit attributable to NCI</p><p>PHE 2024: 3,121,035 = 3,001,475 + 119,560</p></blockquote><h2>2. Measuring NCI at acquisition</h2><p>When a parent buys less than 100 percent of a business, the subsidiary's identifiable assets and liabilities still enter the group at full fair value under PSAK 103 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis</em> (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3). The question is what figure to put on the NCI. PSAK 103 para. 19 gives a choice, made separately for each business combination, for NCI that are present ownership interests entitling holders to a proportionate share of net assets on liquidation:</p><ul><li><p><strong>fair value</strong>, usually the market price of the shares the parent did not buy, or</p></li><li><p><strong>proportionate share</strong> of the acquiree's identifiable net assets.</p></li></ul><p>Goodwill is the residual in both cases.</p><blockquote><p>Goodwill = consideration transferred + NCI + fair value of previously held interest − identifiable net assets at fair value</p></blockquote><p>PHE's policy states that it makes this choice for each combination (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52).</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 60 percent of a listed oilfield-services company for 300,000 cash. The target's book net assets are 350,000. Its equipment is worth 50,000 more than book, with ten years of life left, so identifiable net assets at fair value are 400,000. The 40 percent left in public hands trades at a total market value of 180,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fair value method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Proportionate share method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>180,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>40% × 400,000 = 160,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300,000 + 180,000 − 400,000 = 80,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>300,000 + 160,000 − 400,000 = 60,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Goodwill recognised for</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>parent and NCI ("full goodwill")</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>parent only</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 20,000 gap is the NCI's share of goodwill. The fair value method books it, and the proportionate method leaves it out. Nothing else differs. The elimination entry on the consolidation worksheet under the proportionate method:</p><blockquote><p>Dr Share capital and retained earnings, subsidiary 350,000; Dr Equipment 50,000; Dr Goodwill 60,000 / Cr Investment in subsidiary 300,000; Cr NCI 160,000</p></blockquote><p>The lecture notes measure NCI by grossing up the price, as Beams does: "Implied fair value of sun ($10.200.000÷90%)", so NCI is 10 percent of that implied value. PSAK 103 asks for the NCI's own fair value instead, because the parent's price per share usually includes a control premium that the outside shares do not carry. Grossing up the illustration gives 300,000 / 60% = 500,000, NCI of 200,000 and goodwill of 100,000, which overstates both by 20,000 against the market price of the minority shares.</p><p>Acquisition costs are expensed under either method (Note 3d). Goodwill itself and its impairment are covered in the Intangible Assets essay.</p><h2>3. Profit, OCI and dividends after acquisition</h2><p>After acquisition NCI moves with three things: its share of the subsidiary's profit after consolidation adjustments, its share of OCI, and dividends paid to the outside owners.</p><blockquote><p>NCI at end = NCI at start + NCI share of profit + NCI share of OCI − dividends to NCI ± changes in ownership</p></blockquote><p>The share of profit is computed on the subsidiary's profit <em>as the group sees it</em>. Extra depreciation on fair value uplifts reduces it. So do unrealised profits on upstream sales (a subsidiary selling to its parent), which the notes for AKL Chapter 5 allocate between parent and NCI, while downstream profits fall wholly on the parent.</p><p>For example (illustration), continue the section 2 subsidiary under the proportionate method. In its first year it reports profit of 70,000, pays dividends of 30,000, and records a translation loss in OCI of 10,000. The equipment uplift of 50,000 depreciates over ten years.</p><blockquote><p>Group-basis profit = 70,000 − 50,000 / 10 = 65,000</p><p>NCI share of profit = 40% × 65,000 = 26,000; parent share = 60% × 65,000 = 39,000</p><p>NCI share of OCI = 40% × (10,000) = (4,000)</p><p>Dividends to NCI = 40% × 30,000 = 12,000</p><p>NCI at end = 160,000 + 26,000 − 4,000 − 12,000 = 170,000</p></blockquote><p>The check is that NCI equals 40 percent of the subsidiary's net assets at group carrying amounts, 400,000 + 65,000 − 10,000 − 30,000 = 425,000, and 40% × 425,000 = 170,000. Under the fair value method NCI would be 190,000, the same 20,000 of goodwill above it.</p><p>The worksheet entries follow the order in the notes for AKL Chapter 4, with the parent using the equity method in its own books:</p><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Step</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1. Remove the parent's equity-method income, OCI share and dividends</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Income from subsidiary 39,000 / Cr Dividends 18,000; Cr Investment in subsidiary 21,000; and Dr Investment in subsidiary 6,000 / Cr Translation reserve 6,000 to reverse the parent's own OCI entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2. Record NCI's share</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr NCI share of profit 26,000 / Cr Dividends 12,000; Cr NCI 14,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3. Eliminate opening equity, recognise uplift, goodwill and opening NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>the section 2 entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4. Depreciate the uplift</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Depreciation expense 5,000 / Cr Equipment 5,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5. Attribute OCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr NCI 4,000 / Cr Translation reserve 4,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The parent's investment closes at 300,000 + 39,000 − 18,000 − 6,000 = 315,000, and steps 1 and 3 remove 300,000 + 21,000 − 6,000 = 315,000 of it. Step 5 then puts the translation loss into the group once, 6,000 to the parent and 4,000 to NCI.</p><p><strong>PHE's numbers.</strong> PHE attributes 119,560 of profit and 111,073 of total comprehensive income to NCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15), so NCI's share of OCI was 111,073 − 119,560 = (8,487). The group's whole OCI was 3,083,675 − 3,121,035 = (37,360).</p><blockquote><p>NCI share of profit = 119,560 / 3,121,035 = 3.8%; NCI share of OCI = 8,487 / 37,360 = 22.7%</p></blockquote><p>NCI took a far larger share of the OCI loss than of profit. Elnusa, 51.10 percent owned (Note 1b, Daftar 5/6, PDF p. 23), is the only group entity with the rupiah as functional currency (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46), so the translation loss of 14,810 is its loss.</p><blockquote><p>NCI share of translation loss = 48.90% × 14,810 = 7,242, about 85% of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487</p></blockquote><p>The hedge loss of 21,940 arises in near-wholly-owned companies, so NCI takes little of it. PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary.</p><h2>4. Losses that exceed the NCI</h2><p>Losses are attributed to NCI even when this drives NCI into deficit (PSAK 110 para. B94). PHE's policy says so in the same words (Note 3c, Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51). The older rule in PSAK 4 and pre-2008 IAS 27 charged the excess to the parent unless the minority had a binding obligation to fund it, and later profits went to the parent until the losses it had absorbed were recovered.</p><p>For example (illustration), a 70 percent-owned subsidiary has net assets of 20,000, so NCI is 30% × 20,000 = 6,000. It then loses 50,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Current rule (PSAK 110)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Old rule (PSAK 4, pre-2008 IAS 27)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loss attributed to NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30% × 50,000 = 15,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000, capped at the NCI balance</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loss attributed to parent</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>70% × 50,000 = 35,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>50,000 − 6,000 = 44,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI after the loss</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>6,000 − 15,000 = (9,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A deficit NCI is the outside owners' share of a subsidiary whose liabilities exceed its assets. It does not mean they owe the group anything.</p><h2>5. Changes in ownership without loss of control</h2><p>PSAK 110 para. 23 treats any change in the parent's ownership that leaves control intact as a transaction with owners in their capacity as owners. Once the group view is taken, the group has not sold or bought any asset. Shares in the group have changed hands between two classes of its owners. So:</p><ul><li><p>no gain or loss is recognised and goodwill is not remeasured;</p></li><li><p>NCI is adjusted to reflect the new relative interests;</p></li><li><p>the difference between the consideration and the NCI adjustment goes directly to equity attributable to the parent (para. B96).</p></li></ul><p>The notes for AKL Chapter 8 state the same rule: if control is kept, the sale "akan diperlukan sebagai equity transaction. Tidak ada gain or losses yang diakui." They record the difference in additional paid-in capital, a US practice. PSAK names no equity account; Indonesian groups commonly use an "other equity component" or a reserve for transactions with NCI, and PHE's statement of changes in equity has a column for "equity adjustments (other equity component)" (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16).</p><p>For example (illustration), take the section 3 subsidiary at the start of its second year: net assets at group carrying amounts 425,000, parent 60 percent, NCI 170,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(a) Parent sells 5% for 25,000</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(b) Parent buys 20% from NCI for 80,000</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Ownership after</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>55% parent, 45% NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>80% parent, 20% NCI</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI adjustment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>+5% × 425,000 = +21,250</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>−20% × 425,000 = −85,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Difference to parent's equity</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>25,000 − 21,250 = 3,750 credit</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>85,000 − 80,000 = 5,000 credit</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 25,000 / Cr NCI 21,250; Cr Other equity 3,750</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr NCI 85,000 / Cr Cash 80,000; Cr Other equity 5,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Under the proportionate method the NCI adjustment above uses net assets only. Whether a share of goodwill should also move to or from NCI is a policy question that PSAK 110 does not settle, and practice differs.</p><p>In the parent's separate statements, with the investment at cost under PSAK 227 (IAS 27), sale (a) shows no gain, because the 5 percent cost 300,000 × 5/60 = 25,000. At any other price the gain or loss there would be moved into equity on consolidation.</p><p><strong>The subsidiary issues shares to outsiders.</strong> The parent's percentage falls without the parent selling anything. The notes use a subsidiary with 10,000 shares and equity of 200,000, 80 percent held by the parent, issuing 2,000 new shares to outside investors at 35 each.</p><blockquote><p>Equity after = 200,000 + 2,000 × 35 = 270,000; parent's share = 8,000 / 12,000 = 66.67%</p><p>Parent's interest = 66.67% × 270,000 = 180,000, against 80% × 200,000 = 160,000 before</p><p>NCI = 33.33% × 270,000 = 90,000, against 40,000 before</p><p>Consolidated entry: Dr Cash 70,000 / Cr NCI 50,000; Cr Other equity 20,000</p></blockquote><p>Issuing below book value moves equity the other way. At 15 per share the notes find a decrease of 6,667. A subsidiary buying back its own shares from outside holders works in reverse. The parent's percentage rises, NCI falls by the share bought back, and any difference again stays in equity.</p><p>Accumulated OCI, such as a translation reserve, is reallocated to the new percentages (PSAK 221 para. 48C; see the OCI essay).</p><h2>6. Gaining and losing control</h2><p><strong>Control obtained in stages.</strong> When a parent that already owns part of an investee gains control, the earlier holding is remeasured to fair value at the acquisition date and the gain or loss goes to profit or loss (PSAK 103 para. 42). PHE's policy says this (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52). The notes for AKL Chapter 8 give an example: 5 percent bought for 7,000 and another 5 percent for 8,000, then 80 percent for 210,000.</p><blockquote><p>Implied value of 100% = 210,000 / 80% = 262,500</p><p>Fair value of the earlier 10% = 10% × 262,500 = 26,250; carrying amount = 7,000 + 8,000 = 15,000</p><p>Gain on remeasurement = 26,250 − 15,000 = 11,250</p></blockquote><p>The notes write the calculation as "($262.500 -$7.000 - $8.000)", which does not give 11,250. The steps follow the notes in carrying the earlier 10 percent at cost and valuing it from the parent's price. Under PSAK 109 the stake would already be at fair value, so the para. 42 gain is only the movement since the last reporting date, and it stays in OCI if the FVOCI election was made. Valuing it at 10% of 262,500 also assumes no control premium, the simplification section 2 criticised.</p><p><strong>Control lost.</strong> When the parent loses control it derecognises the subsidiary's assets (including goodwill) and liabilities and the NCI, recognises the consideration and any retained interest at fair value, and takes the difference to profit or loss (PSAK 110 paras. 25 and B98–B99). Amounts in OCI are reclassified as if the parent had disposed of the assets directly, so the translation reserve is recycled.</p><blockquote><p>Gain or loss = (fair value of consideration + fair value of retained interest + carrying amount of NCI) − (carrying amount of net assets + goodwill)</p></blockquote><p>The notes give this formula under the heading "Sale of an interest resulting in consolidation"; the event is a <em>de</em>consolidation.</p><p>For example (illustration), back in the section 3 subsidiary with the parent at 60 percent: net assets 425,000, goodwill 60,000, NCI 170,000, and the parent's share of the translation reserve 60% × (10,000) = (6,000). The parent sells 40 percent for 230,000 and keeps 20 percent, now an associate, with a fair value of 115,000.</p><blockquote><p>Gain = (230,000 + 115,000 + 170,000) − (425,000 + 60,000) = 515,000 − 485,000 = 30,000</p><p>Translation reserve recycled = (6,000); net gain in profit or loss = 24,000</p><p>Dr Cash 230,000; Dr Investment in associate 115,000; Dr NCI 170,000 / Cr Net assets of subsidiary 425,000; Cr Goodwill 60,000; Cr Gain on loss of control 30,000</p><p>Dr Loss on reclassification of translation reserve 6,000 / Cr Translation reserve 6,000</p></blockquote><p>The 115,000 becomes the cost of the associate under the equity method. The Long-Term Investments essay covers that method.</p><h2>7. Indirect holdings</h2><p>In a chain of ownership the NCI in a grandchild has two layers, its own outside owners and the outside owners of the intermediate company. The notes for AKL Chapter 9 recommend an allocation schedule worked from the bottom up: each company first takes its share of the company below it, then passes its own profit up.</p><p>PHE has two such chains in Note 1. Elnusa, 51.10 percent owned, owns subsidiaries such as PT Elnusa Petrofin at 99.99 percent. Etablissements Maurel et Prom (M&amp;P), listed in Paris, is 71.09 percent owned by PT Pertamina Internasional Eksplorasi dan Produksi (PIEP), which PHE owns at 99.999998 percent (Note 1b, Daftar 5/4, 5/6 and 5/8, PDF pp. 21, 23, 25).</p><blockquote><p>PHE's effective interest in Elnusa Petrofin = 51.10% × 99.99% = 51.09%</p><p>PHE's effective interest in M&amp;P = 99.999998% × 71.09% = 71.09%</p></blockquote><p>For example (illustration), suppose Elnusa earns 20,000 from its own operations and Petrofin earns 10,000.</p><blockquote><p>Elnusa level: attributable to Elnusa's owners = 20,000 + 99.99% × 10,000 = 29,999; NCI in Petrofin = 0.01% × 10,000 = 1</p><p>PHE level: attributable to PHE = 51.10% × 29,999 = 15,329.49</p><p>Total NCI = 1 + 48.90% × 29,999 = 1 + 14,669.51 = 14,670.51</p><p>Check: 15,329.49 + 14,670.51 = 30,000</p></blockquote><h2>8. Presentation and disclosure</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Statement of financial position:</strong> a separate line within equity (PSAK 110 para. 22; PSAK 201 para. 54(q), formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1).</p></li><li><p><strong>Statement of profit or loss and OCI:</strong> profit and total comprehensive income each split between owners of the parent and NCI (PSAK 201 para. 81B).</p></li><li><p><strong>Statement of changes in equity:</strong> a column reconciling opening and closing NCI.</p></li><li><p><strong>Statement of cash flows:</strong> dividends paid to NCI are financing cash flows, as the notes for AKL Chapter 4 say. Cash paid or received in changes of ownership without loss of control is also financing (PSAK 207 para. 42A, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7). Only obtaining or losing control is investing (para. 39).</p></li></ul><p>PSAK 112 <em>Pengungkapan Kepentingan dalam Entitas Lain</em> (formerly PSAK 67, IFRS 12) paras. 12 and B10–B11 ask, for each subsidiary with NCI material to the group, for its name and principal place of business, the NCI's ownership and voting share, the profit allocated to NCI, accumulated NCI, dividends paid to NCI and summarised financial information.</p><h2>9. Non-controlling interest across industries</h2><p><strong>Listed holding groups.</strong> Indonesian conglomerates often hold majority stakes in subsidiaries that are themselves listed, so the outside shareholders of each listed child sit in the parent's NCI. Consolidated profit and profit attributable to the parent can then diverge widely, and analysts work from the attributable figures.</p><p><strong>Banks and sharia units.</strong> A conventional bank's sharia business unit (<em>unit usaha syariah</em>, UUS) is a division, so it has no NCI. A spin-off into a separate sharia bank (<em>bank umum syariah</em>) creates a subsidiary, and NCI appears if outside investors subscribe. UU 4/2023 on financial-sector development (P2SK) left the spin-off conditions to OJK (art. 68), and POJK 12/2023 requires a spin-off within two years once the unit's assets reach 50 percent of the parent bank's assets and/or IDR 50 trillion. Inside a sharia subsidiary, temporary <em>syirkah</em> funds (profit-sharing deposits) sit between liabilities and equity under the sharia presentation standard and are not NCI.</p><p><strong>Consolidated funds.</strong> An insurer or asset manager that controls a mutual fund consolidates it. The outside unitholders can redeem their units for cash, so their interest is a financial liability in the consolidated statements, even if the fund itself presents the units as equity (PSAK 232, IAS 32, para. AG29A). For example (illustration), an insurer holds 70 percent of a fund with net asset value of 100,000.</p><blockquote><p>Liability to outside unitholders = 30% × 100,000 = 30,000</p><p>Fund earns 8,000: Dr Change in third-party unitholders' interest (expense) 2,400 / Cr Liability to unitholders 2,400</p></blockquote><p>The 2,400 reduces consolidated profit, unlike an NCI share, which is only an attribution.</p><p><strong>Put options written over NCI.</strong> A parent that promises to buy the outside owners' shares on demand records a liability for the present value of the redemption amount (PSAK 232 para. 23), because it has a contract to deliver cash.</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's NCI</h2><p>PHE has no note for NCI. The movement comes from the statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NCI at 1 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>644,429</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>539,466</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Profit attributable to NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>119,560</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>125,852</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>OCI attributable to NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(8,487)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,008)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dividends declared to NCI</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(27,797)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(18,881)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Equity adjustment in subsidiaries</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(53,470)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>NCI at 31 December</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>674,235</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>644,429</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Note 1 shows where the outside owners sit (Daftar 5/4–5/8, PDF pp. 21–25): 48.90 percent of Elnusa (IDX: ELSA); 28.91 percent of M&amp;P through PIEP; 45 percent of PT Badak NGL (55 percent owned); and stakes of 0.000002 to 1 percent in dozens of companies held at 99 percent or more, including Elnusa's own subsidiaries. M&amp;P's own NCI in its subsidiaries also enters PHE's NCI. PHE gives no split of the 674,235 by subsidiary.</p><blockquote><p>NCI return = 119,560 / ((644,429 + 674,235) / 2) = 119,560 / 659,332 = 18.1%</p><p>Parent return = 3,001,475 / ((13,534,126 + 14,433,117) / 2) = 3,001,475 / 13,983,622 = 21.5%</p><p>Dividends declared in 2024 against 2023 profit: NCI 27,797 / 125,852 = 22.1%; parent 2,079,749 / 2,599,686 = 80.0%</p></blockquote><p>PHE's single shareholder group takes 80 percent of profit as dividends (Note 34, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), while the subsidiaries with outside owners, mostly listed companies, distribute far less of theirs.</p><p>The 2024 "equity adjustment in subsidiaries" is the one ownership event in the line. NCI fell by 53,470, the parent's equity rose by 6,138 (6,140 in other equity component, less 2 in retained earnings), and total equity fell by 47,332. That is arithmetically what section 5(b) produces: buying out outside owners carried at 53,470 for 47,332 would credit the parent with 6,138. No such transaction or cash flow is disclosed. Note 42 reports a gain on sale of subsidiaries of 34,242 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167) and the cash flow statement a net investing receipt of 64,578 from sales and additions of investments in subsidiaries (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). Those more likely relate to TPDC's exercise in January 2024 of its call option over M&amp;P's Mnazi Bay interest in Tanzania, which cut M&amp;P's share from 80 to 60 percent (Note 5g, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); a sale to an outsider would not reduce NCI.</p><h2>11. Upstream note: partners are not NCI</h2><p>Most of PHE's blocks are shared with other contractors. Those partners hold participating interests in a production sharing contract, which is a joint arrangement, so each party records its own share of assets, liabilities, revenue and costs. Their shares never enter PHE's NCI. The many companies held at 99 percent fit Indonesian company law, which requires a limited company to be founded by at least two persons and to restore two shareholders within six months if it falls to one (UU 40/2007 art. 7(1) and 7(5)–(6)).</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/share-capital">share-capital</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/share-capital">additional-paid-in-capital</a>: the parent's contributed equity, and common-control restructurings.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/share-capital">other-equity-component</a>: where differences from transactions with NCI may sit.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/retained-earnings">retained-earnings</a>: dividends to the parent's owners and the profit attributable to them.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income">other-comprehensive-income</a>: translation and hedge reserves, their attribution and recycling.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments">long-term-investments</a>: associates and joint ventures, including a retained interest after loss of control.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net">intangible-assets-net</a>: goodwill on business combinations.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The 2024 equity adjustment (NCI −53,470, parent +6,138) has no disclosed transaction or cash flow. Reading it as a purchase or redemption of NCI shares rests on the numbers alone, and any cash paid would sit in an undisclosed financing line. Linking the investing receipt and the Note 42 gain to TPDC's call option (Note 5g) is also inference; PHE does not connect them.</p></li><li><p>Elnusa's translation explains about 7,242 of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487. The other 1,245 is unexplained (hedge losses in subsidiaries with small outside stakes, or associates' OCI, are candidates); PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary.</p></li><li><p>No PSAK 112 para. 12 table of subsidiaries with material NCI was found in PHE's FS (searched for NCI, KNP and the subsidiaries' names). With 674,235 of NCI spread over Elnusa, M&amp;P and Badak NGL, at least one is likely material, so the disclosure is either elsewhere or missing.</p></li><li><p>Who holds the 1 percent stakes in PHE's 99 percent subsidiaries is not disclosed. If they are held by other PHE companies they are not NCI at all. PT Pertamina Pedeve Indonesia holds 0.0032 percent of PHE itself (Note 32, Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164), which makes it the likely nominee; if so, the stakes are NCI in PHE's statements though inside Pertamina's group. The ownership percentages of M&amp;P (71.09 percent) and Elnusa (about 51 percent) were checked only against secondary and older sources.</p></li><li><p>The banking rules in section 9 (P2SK art. 68, POJK 12/2023 thresholds and two-year window) were seen only in news reports, and a revision of P2SK was under discussion in 2026. The presentation of temporary syirkah funds on consolidation follow general practice, not a checked rule.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15); statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1b (Daftar 5/4–5/8, PDF pp. 21–25); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3c (Daftar 5/33–5/34, PDF pp. 50–51); Note 3d (Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52); Note 5g (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 32 (Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164); Note 34 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk). Figures cross-checked to the PHE FS 2024 data extract.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this page follows, "AKL – Chapter 3 – A Introduction to Consolidated Financial Statements", "AKL – Chapter 4 – Consolidation Technique and Procedures", "AKL – Chapter 5 – Intercompany Profit Transactions: Inventories", "AKL – Chapter 8 – Consolidation: Change in Ownership Interest" and "AKL – Chapter 9 – Indirect and Mutual Holding" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), summarising Beams et al., <em>Advanced Accounting</em>. NOTES. Corrections made here: the notes test control at more than 50 percent (PSAK 110 control model), keep the old PSAK 4 exclusions, first place NCI under liabilities, measure NCI from the implied value of the parent's price (PSAK 103 uses NCI's own fair value), write the step-acquisition gain as "($262.500 −$7.000 − $8.000)", and head the loss-of-control case "resulting in consolidation". Chapter 3's one-year-later elimination entry also reads "Retained earnings $20.000 / Goodwill $40.000" while the worksheet shows goodwill of 20,000; with the subsidiary's year-end retained earnings of 40,000 the entry should be Dr Retained earnings 40,000, Dr Goodwill 20,000. Chapter 8 gives the opening investment as $228.000 in the text and $288.000 in the schedule; 288,000 is consistent with 90% × 300,000 + 18,000. Chapter 8's investment schedule also reads "Net Income (80% x $32.000) $28.800" where the subsidiary's profit is 36,000. Section 9 has no note.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 110 <em>Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian</em> (IFRS 10): paras. 22, 23, 25 VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 text, standards.aasb.gov.au); B96 VERIFIED (web, secondary: Grant Thornton Malaysia, MFRS hot topic Nov 2016); B94 deficit rule VERIFIED (web, secondary: IASB 2008 IAS 27 amendment summaries) and stated in PHE's Note 3c; B98–B99 and the definition in Appendix A STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 103 <em>Kombinasi Bisnis</em> (IFRS 3), para. 19 measurement choice VERIFIED (web, IFRIC agenda papers 2010; Grant Thornton "Recognising and measuring NCI"; ACCA DipIFR article); para. 42 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 112 <em>Pengungkapan Kepentingan dalam Entitas Lain</em> (IFRS 12), paras. 12 and B10–B11 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision, January 2015; AASB 12 Appendix B). PSAK 207 (IAS 7) paras. 39, 42A VERIFIED (web, ESMA enforcement extract 23; IASB update April 2003).</p></li><li><p>Renumbering PSAK 65 → 110, PSAK 22 → 103, PSAK 67 → 112, sharia PSAK 112 (waqf) → 412: VERIFIED (web, IAI, "Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia"). PSAK 105, 201, 221 para. 48C, 227, 228, 232 paras. 23 and AG29A, PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): numbers from the IAI rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>UU 40/2007 on limited companies, art. 7(1), as amended by the job-creation law (now UU 6/2023): VERIFIED (web, secondary: hukumonline, smartlegal.id); art. 7(5)–(6) NOT VERIFIED. UU 4/2023 (P2SK) art. 68 and POJK 12/2023 on sharia business units (thresholds and two-year window): VERIFIED (web, secondary only: Kontan, Fortune IDN, Bisnis, 2023–2026).</p></li><li><p>Ownership of Elnusa (41.1 percent transferred from Pertamina to PHE and 9.9 percent bought from Dana Pensiun Pertamina in 2021, about 51 percent) VERIFIED (web, Pertamina news release; Kontan). PIEP's 71.09 percent of M&amp;P VERIFIED (web, AMF threshold filing, September 2021). Both match PHE's Note 1.</p></li><li><p>Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, <em>Advanced Accounting</em>, chs. 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidation adds 100 percent of a controlled subsidiary's assets, liabilities, income and expenses, even when the parent owns less than all of it. The non-controlling interest (NCI) is the outside owners' share of that subsidiary's equity. It is equity of the group, presented separately from the parent's owners' equity, and it absorbs its share of profit, other comprehensive income (OCI) and losses, even into a deficit.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At acquisition the acquirer chooses, deal by deal, to measure NCI at fair value or at its proportionate share of the identifiable net assets (PSAK 103). At the acquisition date the choice changes goodwill and NCI by the same amount and nothing else.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"While control lasts, buying from or selling to the NCI is a transaction between owners. No gain or loss is recognised, no new goodwill arises, and the difference goes to the parent's equity. Only gaining or losing control is remeasured through profit or loss.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report non-controlling interest of 674,235 at 31 December 2024 against 644,429 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 4.5 percent of total equity (4.5 percent a year earlier) and 2.2 percent of total assets (2.1 percent). Of the year's profit of 3,121,035, 119,560 is attributed to NCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to measure NCI at acquisition, roll it forward through profit, OCI and dividends, record a change in ownership with and without loss of control, compute NCI in a chain of holdings, and read a group's NCI disclosures.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What a non-controlling interest is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI exists because consolidation is all-or-nothing on the asset side and proportionate on the ownership side. PSAK 110 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 65, IFRS 10) defines it as equity in a subsidiary not attributable, directly or indirectly, to a parent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lecture notes (AKL Chapter 3) explain why the group is reported as one entity. Separate legal companies under one management are one economic unit, so their statements are combined line by line and the parent's investment account is replaced by the subsidiary's assets and liabilities. At 90 percent ownership all of the subsidiary's cash is still consolidated, and the outsiders' 10 percent is shown as a claim inside equity.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Control decides who consolidates.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The notes date from the old PSAK 4 era and say control is usually obtained through \"hak mayoritas (lebih dari 50%)\", with exceptions for indirect holdings. PSAK 110 replaced that with three elements, all of which must be present: power over the investee's relevant activities, exposure or rights to variable returns, and the ability to use that power to affect those returns. PHE's policy repeats them (Note 3c, Daftar 5/33, PDF p. 50) and adds that, with less than a majority of votes, it considers contractual arrangements, other contractual rights and potential voting rights (Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51). Majority ownership is the usual route to control, but the test is the three elements. A 45 percent holder whose remaining shares are spread among thousands of small holders may control; a 60 percent holder whose shareholder agreement gives the 40 percent holder a veto over the budget may not.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The notes' PSAK 4 exclusions, temporary control and restrictions on transferring funds, no longer exist; a subsidiary held for sale is consolidated under PSAK 105 (IFRS 5).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI is equity.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The notes first place NCI \"pada bagian sesi liabilities ... dibawah heading non current liabilities\" and then cite GAAP: NCI \"harus ditampilkan dalam balancesheet sebagai bagian yang terpisah dari komponen equity\", which can be misread as outside equity. The current rule puts it within equity, separately from the parent's owners' equity (PSAK 110 para. 22). The outside shareholders own residual interests that nobody owes them, so they are not a liability. The same logic places NCI's share of profit below profit for the year as an attribution, not as an expense.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit for the year = profit attributable to owners of the parent + profit attributable to NCI","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE 2024: 3,121,035 = 3,001,475 + 119,560","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Measuring NCI at acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When a parent buys less than 100 percent of a business, the subsidiary's identifiable assets and liabilities still enter the group at full fair value under PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 22, IFRS 3). The question is what figure to put on the NCI. PSAK 103 para. 19 gives a choice, made separately for each business combination, for NCI that are present ownership interests entitling holders to a proportionate share of net assets on liquidation:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fair value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":", usually the market price of the shares the parent did not buy, or","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"proportionate share","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" of the acquiree's identifiable net assets.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill is the residual in both cases.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill = consideration transferred + NCI + fair value of previously held interest − identifiable net assets at fair value","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy states that it makes this choice for each combination (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 60 percent of a listed oilfield-services company for 300,000 cash. The target's book net assets are 350,000. Its equipment is worth 50,000 more than book, with ten years of life left, so identifiable net assets at fair value are 400,000. The 40 percent left in public hands trades at a total market value of 180,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proportionate share method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"180,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"40% × 400,000 = 160,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300,000 + 180,000 − 400,000 = 80,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"300,000 + 160,000 − 400,000 = 60,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goodwill recognised for","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"parent and NCI (\"full goodwill\")","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"parent only","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 20,000 gap is the NCI's share of goodwill. The fair value method books it, and the proportionate method leaves it out. Nothing else differs. The elimination entry on the consolidation worksheet under the proportionate method:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Share capital and retained earnings, subsidiary 350,000; Dr Equipment 50,000; Dr Goodwill 60,000 / Cr Investment in subsidiary 300,000; Cr NCI 160,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The lecture notes measure NCI by grossing up the price, as Beams does: \"Implied fair value of sun ($10.200.000÷90%)\", so NCI is 10 percent of that implied value. PSAK 103 asks for the NCI's own fair value instead, because the parent's price per share usually includes a control premium that the outside shares do not carry. Grossing up the illustration gives 300,000 / 60% = 500,000, NCI of 200,000 and goodwill of 100,000, which overstates both by 20,000 against the market price of the minority shares.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition costs are expensed under either method (Note 3d). Goodwill itself and its impairment are covered in the Intangible Assets essay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Profit, OCI and dividends after acquisition","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After acquisition NCI moves with three things: its share of the subsidiary's profit after consolidation adjustments, its share of OCI, and dividends paid to the outside owners.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at end = NCI at start + NCI share of profit + NCI share of OCI − dividends to NCI ± changes in ownership","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The share of profit is computed on the subsidiary's profit ","type":"text"},{"text":"as the group sees it","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Extra depreciation on fair value uplifts reduces it. So do unrealised profits on upstream sales (a subsidiary selling to its parent), which the notes for AKL Chapter 5 allocate between parent and NCI, while downstream profits fall wholly on the parent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), continue the section 2 subsidiary under the proportionate method. In its first year it reports profit of 70,000, pays dividends of 30,000, and records a translation loss in OCI of 10,000. The equipment uplift of 50,000 depreciates over ten years.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group-basis profit = 70,000 − 50,000 / 10 = 65,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of profit = 40% × 65,000 = 26,000; parent share = 60% × 65,000 = 39,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of OCI = 40% × (10,000) = (4,000)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dividends to NCI = 40% × 30,000 = 12,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at end = 160,000 + 26,000 − 4,000 − 12,000 = 170,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The check is that NCI equals 40 percent of the subsidiary's net assets at group carrying amounts, 400,000 + 65,000 − 10,000 − 30,000 = 425,000, and 40% × 425,000 = 170,000. Under the fair value method NCI would be 190,000, the same 20,000 of goodwill above it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The worksheet entries follow the order in the notes for AKL Chapter 4, with the parent using the equity method in its own books:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Step","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Remove the parent's equity-method income, OCI share and dividends","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Income from subsidiary 39,000 / Cr Dividends 18,000; Cr Investment in subsidiary 21,000; and Dr Investment in subsidiary 6,000 / Cr Translation reserve 6,000 to reverse the parent's own OCI entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Record NCI's share","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr NCI share of profit 26,000 / Cr Dividends 12,000; Cr NCI 14,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Eliminate opening equity, recognise uplift, goodwill and opening NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the section 2 entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Depreciate the uplift","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Depreciation expense 5,000 / Cr Equipment 5,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Attribute OCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr NCI 4,000 / Cr Translation reserve 4,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The parent's investment closes at 300,000 + 39,000 − 18,000 − 6,000 = 315,000, and steps 1 and 3 remove 300,000 + 21,000 − 6,000 = 315,000 of it. Step 5 then puts the translation loss into the group once, 6,000 to the parent and 4,000 to NCI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's numbers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PHE attributes 119,560 of profit and 111,073 of total comprehensive income to NCI (Daftar 2/2, PDF p. 15), so NCI's share of OCI was 111,073 − 119,560 = (8,487). The group's whole OCI was 3,083,675 − 3,121,035 = (37,360).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of profit = 119,560 / 3,121,035 = 3.8%; NCI share of OCI = 8,487 / 37,360 = 22.7%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI took a far larger share of the OCI loss than of profit. Elnusa, 51.10 percent owned (Note 1b, Daftar 5/6, PDF p. 23), is the only group entity with the rupiah as functional currency (Note 3a, Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46), so the translation loss of 14,810 is its loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI share of translation loss = 48.90% × 14,810 = 7,242, about 85% of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The hedge loss of 21,940 arises in near-wholly-owned companies, so NCI takes little of it. PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Losses that exceed the NCI","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Losses are attributed to NCI even when this drives NCI into deficit (PSAK 110 para. B94). PHE's policy says so in the same words (Note 3c, Daftar 5/34, PDF p. 51). The older rule in PSAK 4 and pre-2008 IAS 27 charged the excess to the parent unless the minority had a binding obligation to fund it, and later profits went to the parent until the losses it had absorbed were recovered.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a 70 percent-owned subsidiary has net assets of 20,000, so NCI is 30% × 20,000 = 6,000. It then loses 50,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current rule (PSAK 110)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Old rule (PSAK 4, pre-2008 IAS 27)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss attributed to NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30% × 50,000 = 15,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000, capped at the NCI balance","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss attributed to parent","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"70% × 50,000 = 35,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"50,000 − 6,000 = 44,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI after the loss","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,000 − 15,000 = (9,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A deficit NCI is the outside owners' share of a subsidiary whose liabilities exceed its assets. It does not mean they owe the group anything.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Changes in ownership without loss of control","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 110 para. 23 treats any change in the parent's ownership that leaves control intact as a transaction with owners in their capacity as owners. Once the group view is taken, the group has not sold or bought any asset. Shares in the group have changed hands between two classes of its owners. So:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no gain or loss is recognised and goodwill is not remeasured;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI is adjusted to reflect the new relative interests;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the difference between the consideration and the NCI adjustment goes directly to equity attributable to the parent (para. B96).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The notes for AKL Chapter 8 state the same rule: if control is kept, the sale \"akan diperlukan sebagai equity transaction. Tidak ada gain or losses yang diakui.\" They record the difference in additional paid-in capital, a US practice. PSAK names no equity account; Indonesian groups commonly use an \"other equity component\" or a reserve for transactions with NCI, and PHE's statement of changes in equity has a column for \"equity adjustments (other equity component)\" (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), take the section 3 subsidiary at the start of its second year: net assets at group carrying amounts 425,000, parent 60 percent, NCI 170,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(a) Parent sells 5% for 25,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(b) Parent buys 20% from NCI for 80,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ownership after","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"55% parent, 45% NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"80% parent, 20% NCI","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI adjustment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"+5% × 425,000 = +21,250","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"−20% × 425,000 = −85,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Difference to parent's equity","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"25,000 − 21,250 = 3,750 credit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"85,000 − 80,000 = 5,000 credit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 25,000 / Cr NCI 21,250; Cr Other equity 3,750","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr NCI 85,000 / Cr Cash 80,000; Cr Other equity 5,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under the proportionate method the NCI adjustment above uses net assets only. Whether a share of goodwill should also move to or from NCI is a policy question that PSAK 110 does not settle, and practice differs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In the parent's separate statements, with the investment at cost under PSAK 227 (IAS 27), sale (a) shows no gain, because the 5 percent cost 300,000 × 5/60 = 25,000. At any other price the gain or loss there would be moved into equity on consolidation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The subsidiary issues shares to outsiders.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The parent's percentage falls without the parent selling anything. The notes use a subsidiary with 10,000 shares and equity of 200,000, 80 percent held by the parent, issuing 2,000 new shares to outside investors at 35 each.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity after = 200,000 + 2,000 × 35 = 270,000; parent's share = 8,000 / 12,000 = 66.67%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent's interest = 66.67% × 270,000 = 180,000, against 80% × 200,000 = 160,000 before","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI = 33.33% × 270,000 = 90,000, against 40,000 before","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated entry: Dr Cash 70,000 / Cr NCI 50,000; Cr Other equity 20,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issuing below book value moves equity the other way. At 15 per share the notes find a decrease of 6,667. A subsidiary buying back its own shares from outside holders works in reverse. The parent's percentage rises, NCI falls by the share bought back, and any difference again stays in equity.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accumulated OCI, such as a translation reserve, is reallocated to the new percentages (PSAK 221 para. 48C; see the OCI essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Gaining and losing control","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Control obtained in stages.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When a parent that already owns part of an investee gains control, the earlier holding is remeasured to fair value at the acquisition date and the gain or loss goes to profit or loss (PSAK 103 para. 42). PHE's policy says this (Note 3d, Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52). The notes for AKL Chapter 8 give an example: 5 percent bought for 7,000 and another 5 percent for 8,000, then 80 percent for 210,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Implied value of 100% = 210,000 / 80% = 262,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value of the earlier 10% = 10% × 262,500 = 26,250; carrying amount = 7,000 + 8,000 = 15,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain on remeasurement = 26,250 − 15,000 = 11,250","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The notes write the calculation as \"($262.500 -$7.000 - $8.000)\", which does not give 11,250. The steps follow the notes in carrying the earlier 10 percent at cost and valuing it from the parent's price. Under PSAK 109 the stake would already be at fair value, so the para. 42 gain is only the movement since the last reporting date, and it stays in OCI if the FVOCI election was made. Valuing it at 10% of 262,500 also assumes no control premium, the simplification section 2 criticised.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Control lost.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When the parent loses control it derecognises the subsidiary's assets (including goodwill) and liabilities and the NCI, recognises the consideration and any retained interest at fair value, and takes the difference to profit or loss (PSAK 110 paras. 25 and B98–B99). Amounts in OCI are reclassified as if the parent had disposed of the assets directly, so the translation reserve is recycled.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain or loss = (fair value of consideration + fair value of retained interest + carrying amount of NCI) − (carrying amount of net assets + goodwill)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The notes give this formula under the heading \"Sale of an interest resulting in consolidation\"; the event is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"de","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"consolidation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), back in the section 3 subsidiary with the parent at 60 percent: net assets 425,000, goodwill 60,000, NCI 170,000, and the parent's share of the translation reserve 60% × (10,000) = (6,000). The parent sells 40 percent for 230,000 and keeps 20 percent, now an associate, with a fair value of 115,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gain = (230,000 + 115,000 + 170,000) − (425,000 + 60,000) = 515,000 − 485,000 = 30,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Translation reserve recycled = (6,000); net gain in profit or loss = 24,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 230,000; Dr Investment in associate 115,000; Dr NCI 170,000 / Cr Net assets of subsidiary 425,000; Cr Goodwill 60,000; Cr Gain on loss of control 30,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Loss on reclassification of translation reserve 6,000 / Cr Translation reserve 6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 115,000 becomes the cost of the associate under the equity method. The Long-Term Investments essay covers that method.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Indirect holdings","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In a chain of ownership the NCI in a grandchild has two layers, its own outside owners and the outside owners of the intermediate company. The notes for AKL Chapter 9 recommend an allocation schedule worked from the bottom up: each company first takes its share of the company below it, then passes its own profit up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE has two such chains in Note 1. Elnusa, 51.10 percent owned, owns subsidiaries such as PT Elnusa Petrofin at 99.99 percent. Etablissements Maurel et Prom (M&P), listed in Paris, is 71.09 percent owned by PT Pertamina Internasional Eksplorasi dan Produksi (PIEP), which PHE owns at 99.999998 percent (Note 1b, Daftar 5/4, 5/6 and 5/8, PDF pp. 21, 23, 25).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's effective interest in Elnusa Petrofin = 51.10% × 99.99% = 51.09%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's effective interest in M&P = 99.999998% × 71.09% = 71.09%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), suppose Elnusa earns 20,000 from its own operations and Petrofin earns 10,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Elnusa level: attributable to Elnusa's owners = 20,000 + 99.99% × 10,000 = 29,999; NCI in Petrofin = 0.01% × 10,000 = 1","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE level: attributable to PHE = 51.10% × 29,999 = 15,329.49","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total NCI = 1 + 48.90% × 29,999 = 1 + 14,669.51 = 14,670.51","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Check: 15,329.49 + 14,670.51 = 30,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Presentation and disclosure","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Statement of financial position:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" a separate line within equity (PSAK 110 para. 22; PSAK 201 para. 54(q), formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Statement of profit or loss and OCI:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" profit and total comprehensive income each split between owners of the parent and NCI (PSAK 201 para. 81B).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Statement of changes in equity:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" a column reconciling opening and closing NCI.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Statement of cash flows:","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" dividends paid to NCI are financing cash flows, as the notes for AKL Chapter 4 say. Cash paid or received in changes of ownership without loss of control is also financing (PSAK 207 para. 42A, formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7). Only obtaining or losing control is investing (para. 39).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 112 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pengungkapan Kepentingan dalam Entitas Lain","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 67, IFRS 12) paras. 12 and B10–B11 ask, for each subsidiary with NCI material to the group, for its name and principal place of business, the NCI's ownership and voting share, the profit allocated to NCI, accumulated NCI, dividends paid to NCI and summarised financial information.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Non-controlling interest across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Listed holding groups.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian conglomerates often hold majority stakes in subsidiaries that are themselves listed, so the outside shareholders of each listed child sit in the parent's NCI. Consolidated profit and profit attributable to the parent can then diverge widely, and analysts work from the attributable figures.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Banks and sharia units.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A conventional bank's sharia business unit (","type":"text"},{"text":"unit usaha syariah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", UUS) is a division, so it has no NCI. A spin-off into a separate sharia bank (","type":"text"},{"text":"bank umum syariah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") creates a subsidiary, and NCI appears if outside investors subscribe. UU 4/2023 on financial-sector development (P2SK) left the spin-off conditions to OJK (art. 68), and POJK 12/2023 requires a spin-off within two years once the unit's assets reach 50 percent of the parent bank's assets and/or IDR 50 trillion. Inside a sharia subsidiary, temporary ","type":"text"},{"text":"syirkah","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" funds (profit-sharing deposits) sit between liabilities and equity under the sharia presentation standard and are not NCI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consolidated funds.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An insurer or asset manager that controls a mutual fund consolidates it. The outside unitholders can redeem their units for cash, so their interest is a financial liability in the consolidated statements, even if the fund itself presents the units as equity (PSAK 232, IAS 32, para. AG29A). For example (illustration), an insurer holds 70 percent of a fund with net asset value of 100,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liability to outside unitholders = 30% × 100,000 = 30,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fund earns 8,000: Dr Change in third-party unitholders' interest (expense) 2,400 / Cr Liability to unitholders 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2,400 reduces consolidated profit, unlike an NCI share, which is only an attribution.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Put options written over NCI.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A parent that promises to buy the outside owners' shares on demand records a liability for the present value of the redemption amount (PSAK 232 para. 23), because it has a contract to deliver cash.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's NCI","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE has no note for NCI. The movement comes from the statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at 1 January","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"644,429","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"539,466","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit attributable to NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"119,560","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"125,852","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"OCI attributable to NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(8,487)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2,008)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dividends declared to NCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(27,797)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(18,881)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Equity adjustment in subsidiaries","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(53,470)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI at 31 December","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"674,235","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"644,429","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 1 shows where the outside owners sit (Daftar 5/4–5/8, PDF pp. 21–25): 48.90 percent of Elnusa (IDX: ELSA); 28.91 percent of M&P through PIEP; 45 percent of PT Badak NGL (55 percent owned); and stakes of 0.000002 to 1 percent in dozens of companies held at 99 percent or more, including Elnusa's own subsidiaries. M&P's own NCI in its subsidiaries also enters PHE's NCI. PHE gives no split of the 674,235 by subsidiary.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NCI return = 119,560 / ((644,429 + 674,235) / 2) = 119,560 / 659,332 = 18.1%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Parent return = 3,001,475 / ((13,534,126 + 14,433,117) / 2) = 3,001,475 / 13,983,622 = 21.5%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dividends declared in 2024 against 2023 profit: NCI 27,797 / 125,852 = 22.1%; parent 2,079,749 / 2,599,686 = 80.0%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's single shareholder group takes 80 percent of profit as dividends (Note 34, Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165), while the subsidiaries with outside owners, mostly listed companies, distribute far less of theirs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 \"equity adjustment in subsidiaries\" is the one ownership event in the line. NCI fell by 53,470, the parent's equity rose by 6,138 (6,140 in other equity component, less 2 in retained earnings), and total equity fell by 47,332. That is arithmetically what section 5(b) produces: buying out outside owners carried at 53,470 for 47,332 would credit the parent with 6,138. No such transaction or cash flow is disclosed. Note 42 reports a gain on sale of subsidiaries of 34,242 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167) and the cash flow statement a net investing receipt of 64,578 from sales and additions of investments in subsidiaries (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17). Those more likely relate to TPDC's exercise in January 2024 of its call option over M&P's Mnazi Bay interest in Tanzania, which cut M&P's share from 80 to 60 percent (Note 5g, Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); a sale to an outsider would not reduce NCI.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: partners are not NCI","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most of PHE's blocks are shared with other contractors. Those partners hold participating interests in a production sharing contract, which is a joint arrangement, so each party records its own share of assets, liabilities, revenue and costs. Their shares never enter PHE's NCI. The many companies held at 99 percent fit Indonesian company law, which requires a limited company to be founded by at least two persons and to restore two shareholders within six months if it falls to one (UU 40/2007 art. 7(1) and 7(5)–(6)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"share-capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"additional-paid-in-capital","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the parent's contributed equity, and common-control restructurings.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-equity-component","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/share-capital","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where differences from transactions with NCI may sit.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"retained-earnings","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/retained-earnings","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": dividends to the parent's owners and the profit attributable to them.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-comprehensive-income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-comprehensive-income","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": translation and hedge reserves, their attribution and recycling.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long-term-investments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/long-term-investments","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": associates and joint ventures, including a retained interest after loss of control.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"intangible-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/intangible-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": goodwill on business combinations.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2024 equity adjustment (NCI −53,470, parent +6,138) has no disclosed transaction or cash flow. Reading it as a purchase or redemption of NCI shares rests on the numbers alone, and any cash paid would sit in an undisclosed financing line. Linking the investing receipt and the Note 42 gain to TPDC's call option (Note 5g) is also inference; PHE does not connect them.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Elnusa's translation explains about 7,242 of NCI's OCI loss of 8,487. The other 1,245 is unexplained (hedge losses in subsidiaries with small outside stakes, or associates' OCI, are candidates); PHE does not attribute OCI by subsidiary.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No PSAK 112 para. 12 table of subsidiaries with material NCI was found in PHE's FS (searched for NCI, KNP and the subsidiaries' names). With 674,235 of NCI spread over Elnusa, M&P and Badak NGL, at least one is likely material, so the disclosure is either elsewhere or missing.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Who holds the 1 percent stakes in PHE's 99 percent subsidiaries is not disclosed. If they are held by other PHE companies they are not NCI at all. PT Pertamina Pedeve Indonesia holds 0.0032 percent of PHE itself (Note 32, Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164), which makes it the likely nominee; if so, the stakes are NCI in PHE's statements though inside Pertamina's group. The ownership percentages of M&P (71.09 percent) and Elnusa (about 51 percent) were checked only against secondary and older sources.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The banking rules in section 9 (P2SK art. 68, POJK 12/2023 thresholds and two-year window) were seen only in news reports, and a revision of P2SK was under discussion in 2026. The presentation of temporary syirkah funds on consolidation follow general practice, not a checked rule.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/3, PDF p. 13); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1–2/2, PDF pp. 14–15); statement of changes in equity (Daftar 3, PDF p. 16); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 1b (Daftar 5/4–5/8, PDF pp. 21–25); Note 3a (Daftar 5/29, PDF p. 46); Note 3c (Daftar 5/33–5/34, PDF pp. 50–51); Note 3d (Daftar 5/35, PDF p. 52); Note 5g (Daftar 5/88, PDF p. 105); Note 32 (Daftar 5/147, PDF p. 164); Note 34 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 42 (Daftar 5/150, PDF p. 167). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk). Figures cross-checked to the PHE FS 2024 data extract.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this page follows, \"AKL – Chapter 3 – A Introduction to Consolidated Financial Statements\", \"AKL – Chapter 4 – Consolidation Technique and Procedures\", \"AKL – Chapter 5 – Intercompany Profit Transactions: Inventories\", \"AKL – Chapter 8 – Consolidation: Change in Ownership Interest\" and \"AKL – Chapter 9 – Indirect and Mutual Holding\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), summarising Beams et al., ","type":"text"},{"text":"Advanced Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". NOTES. Corrections made here: the notes test control at more than 50 percent (PSAK 110 control model), keep the old PSAK 4 exclusions, first place NCI under liabilities, measure NCI from the implied value of the parent's price (PSAK 103 uses NCI's own fair value), write the step-acquisition gain as \"($262.500 −$7.000 − $8.000)\", and head the loss-of-control case \"resulting in consolidation\". Chapter 3's one-year-later elimination entry also reads \"Retained earnings $20.000 / Goodwill $40.000\" while the worksheet shows goodwill of 20,000; with the subsidiary's year-end retained earnings of 40,000 the entry should be Dr Retained earnings 40,000, Dr Goodwill 20,000. Chapter 8 gives the opening investment as $228.000 in the text and $288.000 in the schedule; 288,000 is consistent with 90% × 300,000 + 18,000. Chapter 8's investment schedule also reads \"Net Income (80% x $32.000) $28.800\" where the subsidiary's profit is 36,000. Section 9 has no note.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 110 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Keuangan Konsolidasian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 10): paras. 22, 23, 25 VERIFIED (web, AASB 10 text, standards.aasb.gov.au); B96 VERIFIED (web, secondary: Grant Thornton Malaysia, MFRS hot topic Nov 2016); B94 deficit rule VERIFIED (web, secondary: IASB 2008 IAS 27 amendment summaries) and stated in PHE's Note 3c; B98–B99 and the definition in Appendix A STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 103 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kombinasi Bisnis","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 3), para. 19 measurement choice VERIFIED (web, IFRIC agenda papers 2010; Grant Thornton \"Recognising and measuring NCI\"; ACCA DipIFR article); para. 42 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 112 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pengungkapan Kepentingan dalam Entitas Lain","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 12), paras. 12 and B10–B11 VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision, January 2015; AASB 12 Appendix B). PSAK 207 (IAS 7) paras. 39, 42A VERIFIED (web, ESMA enforcement extract 23; IASB update April 2003).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Renumbering PSAK 65 → 110, PSAK 22 → 103, PSAK 67 → 112, sharia PSAK 112 (waqf) → 412: VERIFIED (web, IAI, \"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia\"). PSAK 105, 201, 221 para. 48C, 227, 228, 232 paras. 23 and AG29A, PSAK 109 (IFRS 9): numbers from the IAI rule (IFRS n → PSAK 100+n, IAS n → PSAK 200+n); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"UU 40/2007 on limited companies, art. 7(1), as amended by the job-creation law (now UU 6/2023): VERIFIED (web, secondary: hukumonline, smartlegal.id); art. 7(5)–(6) NOT VERIFIED. UU 4/2023 (P2SK) art. 68 and POJK 12/2023 on sharia business units (thresholds and two-year window): VERIFIED (web, secondary only: Kontan, Fortune IDN, Bisnis, 2023–2026).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ownership of Elnusa (41.1 percent transferred from Pertamina to PHE and 9.9 percent bought from Dana Pensiun Pertamina in 2021, about 51 percent) VERIFIED (web, Pertamina news release; Kontan). PIEP's 71.09 percent of M&P VERIFIED (web, AMF threshold filing, September 2021). Both match PHE's Note 1.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Beams, Anthony, Bettinghaus and Smith, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Advanced Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", chs. 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of non-controlling interests from the control test to loss of control, worked through PHE's partly owned subsidiaries and set beside the industries where outside owners take other forms","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Consolidation adds 100 percent of a controlled subsidiary's assets, liabilities, income and expenses, even when the parent owns less than all of it. The non-controlling interest (NCI) is the outside owners' share of that subsidiary's equity. It is equity of the group, presented separately from the parent's owners' equity, and it absorbs its share of profit, other comprehensive income (OCI) and losses, even into a deficit.","At acquisition the acquirer chooses, deal by deal, to measure NCI at fair value or at its proportionate share of the identifiable net assets (PSAK 103). At the acquisition date the choice changes goodwill and NCI by the same amount and nothing else.","While control lasts, buying from or selling to the NCI is a transaction between owners. No gain or loss is recognised, no new goodwill arises, and the difference goes to the parent's equity. Only gaining or losing control is remeasured through profit or loss."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '2d6391ad-0b0d-472c-a2d9-1db6ecfc014a';
INSERT INTO public.essay_revisions (essay_id, revision_no, change_type, title, snippet, content_json, status, change_summary, changed_by)
SELECT e.id, coalesce((SELECT max(x.revision_no) FROM public.essay_revisions x WHERE x.essay_id = e.id), 0) + 1, 'rollback',
       e.title, e.snippet, e.content_json, e.status, $s$Restored from the snapshot taken before the lecture-notes removal$s$, null
  FROM public.essays e WHERE e.id = '2d6391ad-0b0d-472c-a2d9-1db6ecfc014a';

-- oil-gas-properties-capitalised-cost-and-reserves
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Upstream asset accounting answers four questions in order: which finding costs become an asset, what a field costs to develop, how that cost is released as barrels are produced, and whether the cost left will come back. The cost policy settles the first, PSAK 216 the second, units-of-production depletion the third, and the impairment test of PSAK 236 the fourth.</p></li><li><p>Successful efforts and full cost split the same exploration spending differently. Successful efforts expenses a dry hole at once, while full cost keeps it on the balance sheet and releases it with the successful barrels. Under PSAK 106 full cost survives only inside exploration and evaluation.</p></li><li><p>The cost of a field includes the present value of the cost to remove it. Decommissioning enters the asset on day one, grows each year as a finance cost, and a change in its estimate adjusts the asset's cost, in most cases leaving profit untouched. PHE carries 17,905,149 of oil and gas properties, net, with 2,242,412 of accumulated impairment against them.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report oil and gas properties, net, of 17,905,149 at 31 December 2024 against 16,130,290 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 58.8 percent of total assets and 79.9 percent of non-current assets (52.2 and 76.8 percent a year earlier). This page also covers two profit or loss lines the asset feeds: exploration expenses, 465,422 against 266,573 (Note 36), and depreciation, depletion and amortisation (DD&amp;A), 2,443,000 against 2,856,853 (Notes 12, 16a, 17) (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14).</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to follow upstream spending from exploration to production, record it in journals, compute depletion and a decommissioning provision, build the exploration and DD&amp;A lines from the notes, and see where other industries differ.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Reserves are in million barrels of oil equivalent (MMBOE, gas converted to oil barrels by energy content), so USD thousand per MMBOE equals USD per barrel. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><p>Intermediate texts barely touch exploration accounting or depletion by reserves, so the order is that of an extractive-industries text, with Kieso's chapters on property, intangibles and provisions where they apply.</p><h2>1. What oil and gas properties are</h2><p>Oil and gas properties are the capitalised cost of finding, developing and later removing the wells and facilities that produce hydrocarbons. The line exists because upstream spending buys barrels that come out over decades. Expensing it when paid would show a loss in the years of drilling and pure profit in the years of production, so accrual accounting holds the cost as an asset and releases it as the barrels are sold (Kieso's expense-recognition principle).</p><p>The life of a field has four phases, and each has its own rule.</p><ul><li><p><em>Acquisition</em>: buying the right to explore. Under Indonesian production sharing contracts (PSCs) this includes the signature bonus paid to the government on signing.</p></li><li><p><em>Exploration and evaluation (E&amp;E)</em>: seismic surveys, exploration wells and appraisal wells drilled to measure a discovery. PSAK 106 <em>Exploration for and Evaluation of Mineral Resources</em> (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6) governs this phase.</p></li><li><p><em>Development</em>: production wells, platforms, pipelines and processing plant. These are property, plant and equipment under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16).</p></li><li><p><em>Production</em>: the cost is depleted, and at the end the field is decommissioned under PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37).</p></li></ul><p>PHE's Note 17 mirrors these phases (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126). At the end of 2024 its cost of 41,972,216 sat in wells (26,793,570), installations (10,917,429), signature and production bonuses (1,034,863), and two assets-under-construction accounts, exploration and evaluation wells (946,010) and development wells (2,280,344).</p><p>The line is non-current in full (PSAK 201.66, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). Use sets the boundary with fixed assets (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>); a shore base serves the company generally, a well serves one field.</p><h2>2. Exploration and evaluation: successful efforts and full cost</h2><p>Exploration is a lottery with expensive tickets, and the accounting question is what to do with the losing ones. A company may drill five exploration wells to find one field. Are the four dry holes a cost of the one field, or a loss?</p><p><strong>Two answers.</strong> <em>Successful efforts</em> says a cost is an asset only if it found something. Exploration wells are capitalised while their result is pending; a well that finds proved reserves stays as an asset, and a dry hole is expensed. <em>Full cost</em> says the dry holes were the price of the discovery. Every finding cost in a large pool, often a whole country, is capitalised and depleted against all production from that pool.</p><p>Kieso's research and development rule follows the same logic. Research is expensed because no specific asset can yet be shown, and development is capitalised once feasibility is demonstrated. Geological and geophysical work sits on the research side. Successful efforts applies that test well by well; full cost treats the whole programme as one development.</p><p><strong>What PSAK 106 allows.</strong> IFRS 6 lets each entity choose which expenditures it recognises as E&amp;E assets and apply that policy consistently, from rights to explore and studies to exploratory drilling (para. 9). Development expenditure may not be held as E&amp;E (para. 10). An E&amp;E asset stops being classified as such once technical feasibility and commercial viability are demonstrable, and it must be tested for impairment before it is reclassified (para. 17). So a full-cost-style policy is possible, but only inside E&amp;E. Once a field is developed its cost falls under PSAK 216 and PSAK 236, which work asset by asset or cash-generating unit by unit, and a country-wide pool is no longer allowed.</p><p>PHE uses successful efforts. Geological and geophysical costs, including seismic, are expensed; exploration well costs are capitalised as "assets under construction – exploration and evaluation wells" until it is known whether they found proved reserves; a successful well is tested for impairment and transferred to development wells, and a dry hole is charged to profit (Note 3m(i), Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73).</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary spends 10,000 on seismic over a new block in 2024 and drills two exploration wells. Well A, 40,000, finds proved reserves. Well B, 25,000, finds water.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Successful efforts</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Full cost (US pool)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Seismic</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Exploration expense 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Oil and gas properties, cost pool 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Drilling both wells</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr AUC – E&amp;E wells 65,000 / Cr Cash 65,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Oil and gas properties, cost pool 65,000 / Cr Cash 65,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Well B dry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Exploration expense, dry hole 25,000 / Cr AUC – E&amp;E wells 25,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Well A commercial, after impairment test</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr AUC – development wells 40,000 / Cr AUC – E&amp;E wells 40,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Successful efforts: 2024 expense = 10,000 + 25,000 = 35,000; asset = 40,000</p><p>Full cost: 2024 expense = 0; asset = 75,000</p></blockquote><p>Over the field's life both methods expense the full 75,000, and US full cost moves 35,000 of expense from 2024 into the production years. Under PSAK 106 a full-cost-style policy could hold the 35,000 only while the area is still in E&amp;E. At reclassification the costs face the para. 17 test, and the standard does not say a dry hole may be carried into a field's development cost. Section 8 shows what PHE's exploration expense contains.</p><p><strong>When E&amp;E is impaired.</strong> PSAK 106 replaces the usual impairment indicators with its own list (paras. 19–20): the right to explore has expired or soon will and is not expected to be renewed, no further substantive exploration in the area is budgeted or planned, exploration has not found commercially viable quantities and the entity has decided to stop, or a development is likely but the E&amp;E cost will not be recovered in full. E&amp;E assets may be grouped for the test, but no group may be larger than an operating segment (para. 21).</p><h2>3. Development costs</h2><p>Once a field is declared commercial, its cost is built up the way Kieso builds the cost of any fixed asset, from everything spent to bring the asset to the condition and location needed for use. That means the purchase price of casing, wellheads and equipment, less discounts, plus transport, installation and testing, plus the rig time and services to drill the wells.</p><p><strong>Dry development wells.</strong> A development well that disappoints is still capitalised. PHE's policy capitalises "unsuccessful development wells" into development wells (Note 3m(ii), Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74). The field's reserves are already proved, so a poor development well is treated as part of the cost of draining a known reservoir; a dry exploration well is a search that found nothing.</p><p><strong>Borrowing costs.</strong> Interest paid while a platform is built is part of the price of having it ready, so PSAK 223 (formerly PSAK 26, IAS 23) capitalises the borrowing costs of a <em>qualifying asset</em>; the weighted-average accumulated expenditures method is worked through on the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a> page. PHE capitalised 2,277 of borrowing costs on the Jambaran-Tiung Biru (JTB) gas development financing in 2024, against 11,321 in 2023 (Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126).</p><p><strong>Later spending.</strong> Kieso sorts spending after acquisition into additions, improvements and replacements, which are capitalised because they add future benefit, and ordinary repairs, which are expensed. In a field the same test separates a <em>workover</em> that restores a well's flow, which is repair and maintenance, from a <em>sidetrack</em> or <em>recompletion</em> that reaches new reservoir and adds reserves, which is an improvement.</p><h2>4. Depletion by units of production</h2><p>A field's cost should be released in proportion to the barrels it gives up. Straight-line depreciation would charge the same amount in a year when the field is shut for maintenance as in a year at peak output. The <em>units-of-production</em> (UOP) method charges each barrel the same share of cost.</p><blockquote><p>Depletion for the year = cost to deplete × production in the year / (reserves at year end + production in the year)</p></blockquote><p><em>Cost to deplete</em> is the carrying amount before the year's depletion. The denominator is the reserves at the start of the year, rebuilt from the year-end estimate so that revisions made during the year count.</p><p><strong>Which reserves.</strong> The reserve base must fit the cost being divided. <em>Proved developed</em> reserves can be produced through wells and facilities already in place, so they match cost already spent. <em>Proved undeveloped</em> reserves need further wells; if they are in the base, the <em>future development costs</em> (FDC) to reach them must be added to the cost. PHE depletes production assets "on the basis of proved reserves, from the date of commercial production of the respective field" (Note 3m(iii), Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74), and defines proved reserves as developed plus undeveloped (Note 4b(i), Daftar 5/78, PDF p. 95). The policy does not say whether FDC is added.</p><p>For example (illustration), one PHE field enters 2024 with a cost to deplete of 600,000. It produces 5 MMBOE. At year end its proved developed reserves are 45 MMBOE and its total proved reserves 70 MMBOE, with FDC of 150,000 to drill the undeveloped part.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Proved developed base</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Proved base with FDC</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Rate</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>600,000 / (45 + 5) = 12.00 per BOE</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(600,000 + 150,000) / (70 + 5) = 10.00 per BOE</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024 depletion</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12.00 × 5 = 60,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10.00 × 5 = 50,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Without the FDC, the proved base would give 600,000 / 75 = 8.00 per BOE, spreading today's cost over barrels that still need money to reach. If maintenance had halved output to 2.5 MMBOE, UOP on the developed base would charge 30,000, while straight-line over a ten-year remaining life would still charge 600,000 / 10 = 60,000.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Depreciation, depletion and amortisation 60,000 / Cr Accumulated depletion 60,000</p></blockquote><p>PHE's 2024 depletion of oil and gas properties was 2,187,085 (Note 17; section 8).</p><p><strong>Reserve revisions.</strong> A new reservoir study is new information, so a revision is a change in accounting estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8), applied from the period of the change onward, with no restatement of past depletion. For example (illustration), the field enters 2025 at 600,000 − 60,000 = 540,000 and again produces 5 MMBOE. Its developed reserves would have been 40 MMBOE at the end of 2025, but a study cuts them to 30.</p><blockquote><p>Without the revision: 540,000 / (40 + 5) × 5 = 60,000</p><p>With the revision: 540,000 / (30 + 5) = 15.43 per BOE; × 5 = 77,143</p><p>Extra 2025 depletion = 77,143 − 60,000 = 17,143</p></blockquote><h2>5. Decommissioning</h2><p>Installing a well or platform creates an obligation to plug, remove and restore at the end of field life. Kieso records it as an environmental provision with a matching asset. The removal cost has no future benefit of its own, so it becomes part of the asset's cost. PSAK 237 recognises a provision when there is a present obligation from a past event, an outflow is probable and the amount can be estimated reliably, at the best estimate discounted where the effect is material. PSAK 216.16(c) adds the same amount to the asset.</p><p>Two things happen afterwards. First, the discount <em>unwinds</em>. Each year the provision grows by the discount rate as settlement approaches, and the increase is charged as a finance cost. Second, estimates change. Under ISAK 101 (formerly ISAK 9, IFRIC 1), for an asset carried at cost, a change in the expected cost, its timing or the discount rate is added to or deducted from the asset's cost in the period of the change; a deduction may not exceed the asset's carrying amount, and any excess goes to profit. An addition is a sign the asset may not be recoverable, so the asset is considered for impairment. PHE's policy states all of this (Note 3o, Daftar 5/60, PDF p. 77).</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary installs a wellhead platform on 1 January 2024, expected to be removed at the end of 2038 for 50,000, discounted at 4 percent over 15 years. At the end of 2025 the removal estimate rises to 60,000 after rig rates climb, with 13 years left.</p><blockquote><p>Initial provision = 50,000 / 1.04^15 = 27,763</p><p>2024 unwinding = 27,763 × 4% = 1,111; provision = 28,874</p><p>2025 unwinding = 28,874 × 4% = 1,155; provision before revision = 30,029</p><p>Revised provision = 60,000 / 1.04^13 = 36,034; increase = 36,034 − 30,029 = 6,005</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Event</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Debit</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Credit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 Jan 2024, installation</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Oil and gas properties 27,763</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Provision for decommissioning 27,763</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024 and 2025, unwinding</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Finance cost 1,111 and 1,155</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Provision for decommissioning 1,111 and 1,155</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2025, revised estimate</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Oil and gas properties 6,005</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Provision for decommissioning 6,005</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The 6,005 does not touch 2025 profit. It is depleted over the platform's remaining barrels, and the impairment test of section 6 decides whether the higher cost is still recoverable. When the work is done, any difference between the provision and the amount paid is a gain or loss.</p><p>PHE's provision stood at 3,826,222 at the end of 2024 (Note 29, Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162). Its 2024 accretion of 119,925 on an opening balance of 3,784,917 is 3.17 percent, which suggests the average discount rate across its fields is of that order.</p><h2>6. Impairment</h2><p>Depletion allocates cost without asking whether it will come back, and the impairment test of PSAK 236 (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36) asks. When there is an indication of impairment, the carrying amount of a <em>cash-generating unit</em> (CGU), the smallest group of assets with largely independent cash inflows, is compared with its <em>recoverable amount</em>, the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use. Oil and gas assets rarely generate cash on their own, and PHE treats its participating interest in each block as one CGU (Note 17, Daftar 5/110, PDF p. 127). Its 2024 indicators included a lower crude price assumption, a higher discount rate, a reserve downgrade and a proposed divestment, and it discounted at 10 percent.</p><p>The largest 2024 loss was on the Mahakam block, written down by 140,968 from 1,256,237 to a recoverable amount of 1,115,269 (Note 17, Daftar 5/111, PDF p. 128). A loss can later be reversed, up to the carrying amount the asset would have had without it, which US full cost does not allow (section 7). For the full PSAK 236 test, see the Impairment of non-financial assets essay.</p><h2>7. Oil and gas properties across industries</h2><p><strong>Mining.</strong> PSAK 106 covers all mineral resources, so a coal or nickel miner makes the same E&amp;E policy choice and depletes mine development cost by units of production over ore reserves. Open-pit mining must also strip waste rock to reach ore. Under ISAK 120 (formerly ISAK 29, IFRIC 20) production-phase stripping that yields ore now is inventory cost, and stripping that improves access to ore later is a <em>stripping activity asset</em>, allocated by a production measure. For example (illustration), a miner spends 10,000 to remove 5,000,000 tonnes of waste while mining 1,000,000 tonnes of ore, where the expected ratio for this part of the pit is 3 tonnes of waste per tonne of ore.</p><blockquote><p>Expected waste = 1,000,000 × 3 = 3,000,000 tonnes; excess = 2,000,000 tonnes</p><p>Stripping activity asset = 10,000 × 2,000,000 / 5,000,000 = 4,000; inventory = 6,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>United States full cost and the ceiling test.</strong> US GAAP still allows full cost beyond exploration, with country pools capped at a <em>ceiling</em>, broadly proved reserves' future net cash flows discounted at 10 percent plus unproved property costs, after tax. For example (illustration), a pool with 900,000 of capitalised cost, a discounted proved-reserve value of 800,000 and unproved costs of 50,000 is written down by 900,000 − (800,000 + 50,000) = 50,000, before tax. A ceiling write-down cannot be reversed when prices recover. PSAK 236 allows reversal and tests far smaller units than a country.</p><p><strong>Joint operations.</strong> Most upstream assets are held with partners. Under PSAK 111 <em>Joint Arrangements</em> (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) a party to a <em>joint operation</em> recognises its share of the assets, liabilities, revenue and expenses line by line. PHE applies that rule (Note 3y, Daftar 5/74, PDF p. 91). For example (illustration), if the operator of a block in which PHE holds 30 percent spends 200,000 on development wells, PHE's oil and gas properties rise by 200,000 × 30% = 60,000, funded through cash calls.</p><h2>8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's notes</h2><p>PHE gives the movement table PSAK 216 asks for (Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126).</p><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Accumulated DD&amp;A</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Impairment</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 January</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>37,777,915</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(19,515,235)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,132,390)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>16,130,290</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Additions</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,946,242</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,187,085)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(220,899)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,538,258</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Deductions</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(304,224)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>21,382</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5,350</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(277,492)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Corrections</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>552,283</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(143,717)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>105,527</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>514,093</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>31 December</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>41,972,216</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(21,824,655)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>(2,242,412)</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>17,905,149</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Additions = 183,123 producing assets + 231,940 E&amp;E wells + 3,531,179 development wells = 3,946,242</p><p>Reinvestment ratio = 3,946,242 / 2,187,085 = 1.80 times</p><p>Accumulated DD&amp;A and impairment / cost = (21,824,655 + 2,242,412) / 41,972,216 = 57.3%</p></blockquote><p>Three presentation points need a careful reader. The "corrections" column adds 514,093 net and is not explained in the note. The English text of the impairment paragraph gives the 2024 loss as US$257,554, while the Indonesian text and the table give 220,899 (Daftar 5/110, PDF p. 127), and the Indonesian text is the original. And the decommissioning movement in Note 29 prints the 2024 revision as 78,620 without brackets, although the balance only foots if it is a reduction: 3,784,917 − 78,620 + 119,925 = 3,826,222.</p><p><strong>Exploration expense.</strong> Note 36 shows what successful efforts sends straight to profit or loss (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dry holes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>156,746</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>100,703</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Geological and geophysical</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>126,786</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>77,408</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Administration and exploration</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>123,932</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>68,407</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>57,958</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20,055</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Exploration expenses</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>465,422</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>266,573</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Dry holes are exploration wells written off for finding no proved reserves, like well B in section 2; geological and geophysical costs are seismic and studies. Administration and exploration is presumably the overhead of the exploration function, which PHE does not break down. The line rose 198,849, or 74.6 percent, and dry holes 56,043 of it. Under full cost the dry holes and geological and geophysical costs, 283,532 or 60.9 percent of the line, would sit in the asset, and so would overhead directly tied to exploration, so this is the line where the two methods' profits part.</p><p><strong>DD&amp;A.</strong> The face line is wider than this asset's depletion (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14). Note 46 splits it by segment, exploration and production 2,416,986, services 94,292 and elimination −68,278 (Daftar 5/220, PDF p. 237), but not by asset, so the build below is this page's.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Oil and gas properties (Note 17)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,187,085</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,570,262</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fixed assets (Note 12, Daftar 5/100–5/101)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>74,311</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>68,401</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investments in oil and gas blocks (Note 16a, Daftar 5/107)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>81,381</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Right-of-use assets (Note 18) and intangibles (Note 13)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>120,322</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>134,542</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Not identified (see Least-confident)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>61,282</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,267</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Depreciation, depletion and amortisation</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,443,000</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,856,853</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The line fell 413,853, or 14.5 percent. Depletion of wells fell 383,467 (1,882,700 to 1,499,233) while the cost of producing assets grew. The Malaysian and Iraqi block investments, amortised by 81,381 in 2023, show no 2024 amortisation. Under units of production the charge falls when output falls, when reserves rise, or when the cost to deplete shrinks, and the 2023 impairment of 707,087 cut that cost before 2024 began. The statements give neither volumes nor reserves, so the three causes cannot be separated.</p><blockquote><p>DD&amp;A / opening cost of producing assets = 2,187,085 / 34,452,668 = 6.3% (2023: 2,570,262 / 30,409,868 = 8.5%)</p></blockquote><h2>9. Upstream note: who owns the wells</h2><p>Under a cost-recovery PSC, "materials, supplies and equipment acquired by the PSC Contractors … belongs to the Government", while the contractor may use them until SKK Migas, the upstream regulator, approves them as surplus or abandoned (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). PHE still carries the wells and facilities. An asset under the Conceptual Framework is a resource the entity controls, control is the present ability to direct its use and obtain its benefits, and the 1989 Framework already said that ownership is not essential. A plausible reading is that the contractor directs the use of the wells and recovers their cost, though PHE does not explain why it recognises them.</p><p>Annual cost recovery under the PSC comprises the year's non-capital costs, depreciation of capital costs, and earlier unrecovered costs (Note 2a(iii), Daftar 5/10, PDF p. 27). Book depletion and PSC depreciation follow different schedules, and the gap between them is a temporary difference for deferred tax. Under gross split contracts there is no cost recovery, and the old PSC assets, now owned by the Directorate General of State Assets (DJKN), are used under a lease scheme (Note 2b, Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34).</p><h2>10. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>: the boundary for assets that serve the company generally.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: the liability side of section 5.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/restricted-cash">restricted-cash-non-current</a>: the decommissioning funds held in joint accounts with SKK Migas.</p></li><li><p><code>exploration-expenses</code>, <code>depreciation-depletion-amortization</code>: the profit or loss lines this asset feeds, covered on this page (section 8).</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/impairment-non-financial-assets">impairment-non-financial-assets</a>: the full PSAK 236 test behind section 6.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">advances-prepayments-current</a>: cash calls paid to operators before the spending reaches this line.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/inventories-net">inventories-net</a>: materials issued to development wells.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the gap between book depletion and PSC recovery.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The control argument for carrying government-owned wells is this page's reading; PHE does not give one.</p></li><li><p>PHE's policy says depletion uses "proved reserves" and defines them as developed plus undeveloped; whether PHE adds future development costs to the base is not disclosed, so the FDC treatment shown is the textbook rule and may differ from PHE's practice.</p></li><li><p>The 3.17 percent "average discount rate" is accretion over the opening provision, so only a rough figure. The reading that Note 29's 78,620 is a reduction rests on footing alone; PHE may have omitted a line rather than a bracket.</p></li><li><p>The DD&amp;A build in section 8 is this page's. The face cites Notes 12, 16a and 17, which explain 2,261,396 of 2,443,000 in 2024. Note 18's 2024 depreciation column includes a credit of 59,120 on tanks and pipelines, probably a misplaced derecognition or correction; without it right-of-use depreciation is 166,292 and, with intangible amortisation (Note 13, 13,150), the residual is about 2,162, close to 2023's 2,267. As printed, the residual is 61,282. Note 16a shows no 2024 amortisation of the Malaysian blocks, only a derecognition of 63,703.</p></li><li><p>How far a full-cost-style E&amp;E policy can defer dry-hole cost under PSAK 106 is this page's reading of paras. 9 and 17; no standard or interpretation found says whether a dry hole may enter a field's development cost on reclassification.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 2a(iii) and 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/10–5/11, PDF pp. 27–28); Note 2b (Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34); Note 3m (Daftar 5/56–5/57, PDF pp. 73–74); Note 3o (Daftar 5/60–5/61, PDF pp. 77–78); Note 3w (Daftar 5/72–5/73, PDF pp. 89–90); Note 3y (Daftar 5/74, PDF p. 91); Note 4b(i) (Daftar 5/78–5/79, PDF pp. 95–96); Note 17 (Daftar 5/109–5/111, PDF pp. 126–128); Note 29 (Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162, page image checked); Note 36 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166). Profit or loss lines: statement of profit or loss (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 3p (Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80); Note 12 (Daftar 5/100–5/101, PDF pp. 117–118); Note 13 (Daftar 5/104, PDF p. 121); Note 16a (Daftar 5/107, PDF p. 124); Note 18 (Daftar 5/112, PDF p. 129). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; line figures checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract).</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 1 – Chapter 10 – Acquisition and Disposition of Property, Plant and Equipment" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): cost elements, self-constructed assets and overhead, capitalisation of interest (worked on the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a> page), later spending. NOTES.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 1 – Chapter 12 – Intangible Assets": research versus development, impairment of limited-life assets, reversal capped by the no-impairment carrying amount. NOTES. The notes measure recoverable amount by fair value only; corrected here to the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities, Provisions and Contingencies": recognition of provisions and the environmental provision with a matching asset. NOTES. The notes do not cover exploration accounting, depletion or the extractive standards; sections 2, 4 and 7 follow the standards named.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 1 – Chapter 2 – Conceptual Framework" and "TA – Chapter 7 – Assets": matching, and control over ownership. NOTES. The TA note quotes the FASB, IASC and AASB asset definitions and names no author for the control point. The 1989 Framework's "ownership is not essential" wording VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation material quoting the Framework).</p></li><li><p>Kieso, D. E., Weygandt, J. J. and Warfield, T. D., <em>Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition</em> (Wiley), chapters on acquisition and disposition of property, plant and equipment, intangible assets, and current liabilities, provisions and contingencies; the textbook the notes above follow. Edition not checked. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Godfrey, J., Hodgson, A., Tarca, A., Hamilton, J. and Holmes, S., <em>Accounting Theory</em> (Wiley), the text the TA notes are thought to follow; not cited in the body, because the book is not on disk. NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 106 (IFRS 6), paras. 5, 9, 10, 17–21. VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation text of IFRS 6 reproduced at ktkt.uel.edu.vn; faronline.se for paras. 3 and 5).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 216 (IAS 16), paras. 12 and 16(c). VERIFIED (web, ACCA technical article and study materials quoting the paragraphs).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 223 (IAS 23), capitalisation of borrowing costs on qualifying assets (worked on the <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a> page). VERIFIED (web, IFRS Interpretations Committee staff papers; secondary summaries).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 236 (IAS 36), recoverable amount and the capped reversal (para. 117). VERIFIED (web, KPMG and Grant Thornton guidance on reversals; ACCA article). Worked in full in the Impairment of non-financial assets essay.</p></li><li><p>ISAK 101 (IFRIC 1), paras. 5 and 8. VERIFIED (web, earlier recheck via faronline.se and BDO excerpts; scope reconfirmed via faronline.se 2026-10-11); the full interpretation was not reopened.</p></li><li><p>ISAK 120 (IFRIC 20). Allocation principle VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation IFRIC 20 page and IAS Plus summary); ratio-based example STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1) para. 66; PSAK 208 (IAS 8); PSAK 237 (IAS 37); PSAK 111 (IFRS 11). Numbers and titles VERIFIED (web, IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em>); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>SEC Regulation S-X Rule 4-10 full cost ceiling test. VERIFIED (web, SEC filings by Ultra Petroleum and Anadarko describing the test); rule text not opened, and its tax adjustments are left out of the example.</p></li><li><p>Lessons salvaged from the independent recheck of the superseded draft (2026-10-09), and the v0.5 recheck (2026-10-11).</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream asset accounting answers four questions in order: which finding costs become an asset, what a field costs to develop, how that cost is released as barrels are produced, and whether the cost left will come back. The cost policy settles the first, PSAK 216 the second, units-of-production depletion the third, and the impairment test of PSAK 236 the fourth.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Successful efforts and full cost split the same exploration spending differently. Successful efforts expenses a dry hole at once, while full cost keeps it on the balance sheet and releases it with the successful barrels. Under PSAK 106 full cost survives only inside exploration and evaluation.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cost of a field includes the present value of the cost to remove it. Decommissioning enters the asset on day one, grows each year as a finance cost, and a change in its estimate adjusts the asset's cost, in most cases leaving profit untouched. PHE carries 17,905,149 of oil and gas properties, net, with 2,242,412 of accumulated impairment against them.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report oil and gas properties, net, of 17,905,149 at 31 December 2024 against 16,130,290 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 58.8 percent of total assets and 79.9 percent of non-current assets (52.2 and 76.8 percent a year earlier). This page also covers two profit or loss lines the asset feeds: exploration expenses, 465,422 against 266,573 (Note 36), and depreciation, depletion and amortisation (DD&A), 2,443,000 against 2,856,853 (Notes 12, 16a, 17) (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to follow upstream spending from exploration to production, record it in journals, compute depletion and a decommissioning provision, build the exploration and DD&A lines from the notes, and see where other industries differ.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Reserves are in million barrels of oil equivalent (MMBOE, gas converted to oil barrels by energy content), so USD thousand per MMBOE equals USD per barrel. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Intermediate texts barely touch exploration accounting or depletion by reserves, so the order is that of an extractive-industries text, with Kieso's chapters on property, intangibles and provisions where they apply.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What oil and gas properties are","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Oil and gas properties are the capitalised cost of finding, developing and later removing the wells and facilities that produce hydrocarbons. The line exists because upstream spending buys barrels that come out over decades. Expensing it when paid would show a loss in the years of drilling and pure profit in the years of production, so accrual accounting holds the cost as an asset and releases it as the barrels are sold (Kieso's expense-recognition principle).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The life of a field has four phases, and each has its own rule.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Acquisition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": buying the right to explore. Under Indonesian production sharing contracts (PSCs) this includes the signature bonus paid to the government on signing.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exploration and evaluation (E&E)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": seismic surveys, exploration wells and appraisal wells drilled to measure a discovery. PSAK 106 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Exploration for and Evaluation of Mineral Resources","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 64, IFRS 6) governs this phase.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Development","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": production wells, platforms, pipelines and processing plant. These are property, plant and equipment under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Production","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the cost is depleted, and at the end the field is decommissioned under PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's Note 17 mirrors these phases (Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126). At the end of 2024 its cost of 41,972,216 sat in wells (26,793,570), installations (10,917,429), signature and production bonuses (1,034,863), and two assets-under-construction accounts, exploration and evaluation wells (946,010) and development wells (2,280,344).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The line is non-current in full (PSAK 201.66, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). Use sets the boundary with fixed assets (","type":"text"},{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":"); a shore base serves the company generally, a well serves one field.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Exploration and evaluation: successful efforts and full cost","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exploration is a lottery with expensive tickets, and the accounting question is what to do with the losing ones. A company may drill five exploration wells to find one field. Are the four dry holes a cost of the one field, or a loss?","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two answers.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Successful efforts","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" says a cost is an asset only if it found something. Exploration wells are capitalised while their result is pending; a well that finds proved reserves stays as an asset, and a dry hole is expensed. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Full cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" says the dry holes were the price of the discovery. Every finding cost in a large pool, often a whole country, is capitalised and depleted against all production from that pool.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso's research and development rule follows the same logic. Research is expensed because no specific asset can yet be shown, and development is capitalised once feasibility is demonstrated. Geological and geophysical work sits on the research side. Successful efforts applies that test well by well; full cost treats the whole programme as one development.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What PSAK 106 allows.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" IFRS 6 lets each entity choose which expenditures it recognises as E&E assets and apply that policy consistently, from rights to explore and studies to exploratory drilling (para. 9). Development expenditure may not be held as E&E (para. 10). An E&E asset stops being classified as such once technical feasibility and commercial viability are demonstrable, and it must be tested for impairment before it is reclassified (para. 17). So a full-cost-style policy is possible, but only inside E&E. Once a field is developed its cost falls under PSAK 216 and PSAK 236, which work asset by asset or cash-generating unit by unit, and a country-wide pool is no longer allowed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE uses successful efforts. Geological and geophysical costs, including seismic, are expensed; exploration well costs are capitalised as \"assets under construction – exploration and evaluation wells\" until it is known whether they found proved reserves; a successful well is tested for impairment and transferred to development wells, and a dry hole is charged to profit (Note 3m(i), Daftar 5/56, PDF p. 73).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary spends 10,000 on seismic over a new block in 2024 and drills two exploration wells. Well A, 40,000, finds proved reserves. Well B, 25,000, finds water.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Successful efforts","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Full cost (US pool)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Seismic","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Exploration expense 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Oil and gas properties, cost pool 10,000 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Drilling both wells","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr AUC – E&E wells 65,000 / Cr Cash 65,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Oil and gas properties, cost pool 65,000 / Cr Cash 65,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Well B dry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Exploration expense, dry hole 25,000 / Cr AUC – E&E wells 25,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Well A commercial, after impairment test","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr AUC – development wells 40,000 / Cr AUC – E&E wells 40,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Successful efforts: 2024 expense = 10,000 + 25,000 = 35,000; asset = 40,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Full cost: 2024 expense = 0; asset = 75,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Over the field's life both methods expense the full 75,000, and US full cost moves 35,000 of expense from 2024 into the production years. Under PSAK 106 a full-cost-style policy could hold the 35,000 only while the area is still in E&E. At reclassification the costs face the para. 17 test, and the standard does not say a dry hole may be carried into a field's development cost. Section 8 shows what PHE's exploration expense contains.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When E&E is impaired.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 106 replaces the usual impairment indicators with its own list (paras. 19–20): the right to explore has expired or soon will and is not expected to be renewed, no further substantive exploration in the area is budgeted or planned, exploration has not found commercially viable quantities and the entity has decided to stop, or a development is likely but the E&E cost will not be recovered in full. E&E assets may be grouped for the test, but no group may be larger than an operating segment (para. 21).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Development costs","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Once a field is declared commercial, its cost is built up the way Kieso builds the cost of any fixed asset, from everything spent to bring the asset to the condition and location needed for use. That means the purchase price of casing, wellheads and equipment, less discounts, plus transport, installation and testing, plus the rig time and services to drill the wells.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dry development wells.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A development well that disappoints is still capitalised. PHE's policy capitalises \"unsuccessful development wells\" into development wells (Note 3m(ii), Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74). The field's reserves are already proved, so a poor development well is treated as part of the cost of draining a known reservoir; a dry exploration well is a search that found nothing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Borrowing costs.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Interest paid while a platform is built is part of the price of having it ready, so PSAK 223 (formerly PSAK 26, IAS 23) capitalises the borrowing costs of a ","type":"text"},{"text":"qualifying asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; the weighted-average accumulated expenditures method is worked through on the ","type":"text"},{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" page. PHE capitalised 2,277 of borrowing costs on the Jambaran-Tiung Biru (JTB) gas development financing in 2024, against 11,321 in 2023 (Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Later spending.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Kieso sorts spending after acquisition into additions, improvements and replacements, which are capitalised because they add future benefit, and ordinary repairs, which are expensed. In a field the same test separates a ","type":"text"},{"text":"workover","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" that restores a well's flow, which is repair and maintenance, from a ","type":"text"},{"text":"sidetrack","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" or ","type":"text"},{"text":"recompletion","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" that reaches new reservoir and adds reserves, which is an improvement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Depletion by units of production","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A field's cost should be released in proportion to the barrels it gives up. Straight-line depreciation would charge the same amount in a year when the field is shut for maintenance as in a year at peak output. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"units-of-production","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (UOP) method charges each barrel the same share of cost.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depletion for the year = cost to deplete × production in the year / (reserves at year end + production in the year)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost to deplete","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the carrying amount before the year's depletion. The denominator is the reserves at the start of the year, rebuilt from the year-end estimate so that revisions made during the year count.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Which reserves.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The reserve base must fit the cost being divided. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Proved developed","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" reserves can be produced through wells and facilities already in place, so they match cost already spent. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Proved undeveloped","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" reserves need further wells; if they are in the base, the ","type":"text"},{"text":"future development costs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (FDC) to reach them must be added to the cost. PHE depletes production assets \"on the basis of proved reserves, from the date of commercial production of the respective field\" (Note 3m(iii), Daftar 5/57, PDF p. 74), and defines proved reserves as developed plus undeveloped (Note 4b(i), Daftar 5/78, PDF p. 95). The policy does not say whether FDC is added.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), one PHE field enters 2024 with a cost to deplete of 600,000. It produces 5 MMBOE. At year end its proved developed reserves are 45 MMBOE and its total proved reserves 70 MMBOE, with FDC of 150,000 to drill the undeveloped part.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proved developed base","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proved base with FDC","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rate","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"600,000 / (45 + 5) = 12.00 per BOE","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(600,000 + 150,000) / (70 + 5) = 10.00 per BOE","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 depletion","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12.00 × 5 = 60,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10.00 × 5 = 50,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Without the FDC, the proved base would give 600,000 / 75 = 8.00 per BOE, spreading today's cost over barrels that still need money to reach. If maintenance had halved output to 2.5 MMBOE, UOP on the developed base would charge 30,000, while straight-line over a ten-year remaining life would still charge 600,000 / 10 = 60,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Depreciation, depletion and amortisation 60,000 / Cr Accumulated depletion 60,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's 2024 depletion of oil and gas properties was 2,187,085 (Note 17; section 8).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Reserve revisions.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A new reservoir study is new information, so a revision is a change in accounting estimate under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8), applied from the period of the change onward, with no restatement of past depletion. For example (illustration), the field enters 2025 at 600,000 − 60,000 = 540,000 and again produces 5 MMBOE. Its developed reserves would have been 40 MMBOE at the end of 2025, but a study cuts them to 30.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Without the revision: 540,000 / (40 + 5) × 5 = 60,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"With the revision: 540,000 / (30 + 5) = 15.43 per BOE; × 5 = 77,143","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Extra 2025 depletion = 77,143 − 60,000 = 17,143","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Decommissioning","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Installing a well or platform creates an obligation to plug, remove and restore at the end of field life. Kieso records it as an environmental provision with a matching asset. The removal cost has no future benefit of its own, so it becomes part of the asset's cost. PSAK 237 recognises a provision when there is a present obligation from a past event, an outflow is probable and the amount can be estimated reliably, at the best estimate discounted where the effect is material. PSAK 216.16(c) adds the same amount to the asset.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Two things happen afterwards. First, the discount ","type":"text"},{"text":"unwinds","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". Each year the provision grows by the discount rate as settlement approaches, and the increase is charged as a finance cost. Second, estimates change. Under ISAK 101 (formerly ISAK 9, IFRIC 1), for an asset carried at cost, a change in the expected cost, its timing or the discount rate is added to or deducted from the asset's cost in the period of the change; a deduction may not exceed the asset's carrying amount, and any excess goes to profit. An addition is a sign the asset may not be recoverable, so the asset is considered for impairment. PHE's policy states all of this (Note 3o, Daftar 5/60, PDF p. 77).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary installs a wellhead platform on 1 January 2024, expected to be removed at the end of 2038 for 50,000, discounted at 4 percent over 15 years. At the end of 2025 the removal estimate rises to 60,000 after rig rates climb, with 13 years left.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Initial provision = 50,000 / 1.04^15 = 27,763","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 unwinding = 27,763 × 4% = 1,111; provision = 28,874","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 unwinding = 28,874 × 4% = 1,155; provision before revision = 30,029","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revised provision = 60,000 / 1.04^13 = 36,034; increase = 36,034 − 30,029 = 6,005","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Event","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Debit","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Credit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 Jan 2024, installation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Oil and gas properties 27,763","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision for decommissioning 27,763","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024 and 2025, unwinding","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Finance cost 1,111 and 1,155","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision for decommissioning 1,111 and 1,155","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2025, revised estimate","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Oil and gas properties 6,005","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Provision for decommissioning 6,005","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 6,005 does not touch 2025 profit. It is depleted over the platform's remaining barrels, and the impairment test of section 6 decides whether the higher cost is still recoverable. When the work is done, any difference between the provision and the amount paid is a gain or loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's provision stood at 3,826,222 at the end of 2024 (Note 29, Daftar 5/145, PDF p. 162). Its 2024 accretion of 119,925 on an opening balance of 3,784,917 is 3.17 percent, which suggests the average discount rate across its fields is of that order.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Impairment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Depletion allocates cost without asking whether it will come back, and the impairment test of PSAK 236 (formerly PSAK 48, IAS 36) asks. When there is an indication of impairment, the carrying amount of a ","type":"text"},{"text":"cash-generating unit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (CGU), the smallest group of assets with largely independent cash inflows, is compared with its ","type":"text"},{"text":"recoverable amount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use. Oil and gas assets rarely generate cash on their own, and PHE treats its participating interest in each block as one CGU (Note 17, Daftar 5/110, PDF p. 127). Its 2024 indicators included a lower crude price assumption, a higher discount rate, a reserve downgrade and a proposed divestment, and it discounted at 10 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The largest 2024 loss was on the Mahakam block, written down by 140,968 from 1,256,237 to a recoverable amount of 1,115,269 (Note 17, Daftar 5/111, PDF p. 128). A loss can later be reversed, up to the carrying amount the asset would have had without it, which US full cost does not allow (section 7). For the full PSAK 236 test, see the Impairment of non-financial assets essay.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Oil and gas properties across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mining.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 106 covers all mineral resources, so a coal or nickel miner makes the same E&E policy choice and depletes mine development cost by units of production over ore reserves. Open-pit mining must also strip waste rock to reach ore. Under ISAK 120 (formerly ISAK 29, IFRIC 20) production-phase stripping that yields ore now is inventory cost, and stripping that improves access to ore later is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"stripping activity asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", allocated by a production measure. For example (illustration), a miner spends 10,000 to remove 5,000,000 tonnes of waste while mining 1,000,000 tonnes of ore, where the expected ratio for this part of the pit is 3 tonnes of waste per tonne of ore.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected waste = 1,000,000 × 3 = 3,000,000 tonnes; excess = 2,000,000 tonnes","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stripping activity asset = 10,000 × 2,000,000 / 5,000,000 = 4,000; inventory = 6,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"United States full cost and the ceiling test.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" US GAAP still allows full cost beyond exploration, with country pools capped at a ","type":"text"},{"text":"ceiling","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", broadly proved reserves' future net cash flows discounted at 10 percent plus unproved property costs, after tax. For example (illustration), a pool with 900,000 of capitalised cost, a discounted proved-reserve value of 800,000 and unproved costs of 50,000 is written down by 900,000 − (800,000 + 50,000) = 50,000, before tax. A ceiling write-down cannot be reversed when prices recover. PSAK 236 allows reversal and tests far smaller units than a country.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Joint operations.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Most upstream assets are held with partners. Under PSAK 111 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Joint Arrangements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 66, IFRS 11) a party to a ","type":"text"},{"text":"joint operation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" recognises its share of the assets, liabilities, revenue and expenses line by line. PHE applies that rule (Note 3y, Daftar 5/74, PDF p. 91). For example (illustration), if the operator of a block in which PHE holds 30 percent spends 200,000 on development wells, PHE's oil and gas properties rise by 200,000 × 30% = 60,000, funded through cash calls.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's notes","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE gives the movement table PSAK 216 asks for (Note 17, Daftar 5/109, PDF p. 126).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accumulated DD&A","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Impairment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 January","type":